WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:12.817
Hello and good day everyone.

00:00:12.817 --> 00:00:15.855
My name is Ronie Shilo and
I'm the director of professional programs,

00:00:15.855 --> 00:00:19.120
here at the Stanford Center for
Professional Development.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.630
It's my great pleasure to introduce
today's speaker for our webinar,

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.340
Professor Jeffrey Pfeffer from
the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.980
He is the Thomas Dee D. Professor
of Organizational Behavior and

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.150
has taught in the Graduate School
of Business since 1979.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.480
Jeffrey's the author or co-author of 14
books on the topic, including power and

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.700
organization, managing people,
evidence based management,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.510
and the knowing-doing gap.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:50.440
His latest book, Leadership BS: Fixing Workplaces and Careers, One Truth at a Time,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.865
which we'll be hearing about
today was publish in September 2015.

00:00:54.865 --> 00:00:57.835
Pfeffer is author of more than 150
articles and book chapters, and

00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:00.565
has won numerous awards for
his scholarly research,

00:01:00.565 --> 00:01:05.287
including an honorary doctorate from
Tilburg University in the Netherlands.

00:01:05.287 --> 00:01:07.377
Pfeffer has taught seminars
in 39 countries, and

00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:12.107
has been a visiting professor at Harvard
Business School, London Business School,

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:15.217
Singapore Management University and
IEFE in Barcelona.

00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:19.337
Prior to joining Stanford, he was on
the faculty at the business schools

00:01:19.337 --> 00:01:23.400
at the Universities California Berkeley
and the University of Illinois.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.380
He has served at the board of directors of
several human capital software companies

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.245
as well as other public and
private companies and non-profit boards.

00:01:32.245 --> 00:01:35.380
So definitely very accomplished,
and it should be a great

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.180
pleasure to hear him speak to us today
about his latest book Leadership BS.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.810
So with that I'm going to
hand it over to Jeff.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:47.110
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, it's a pleasure to
be with everybody who's on the webinar.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:52.390
The contact slide that Ronie just went
by had my contact information on it.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:56.770
I would encourage people, if you have
questions not answered by the webinar, or

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.450
if you want to be in touch with me for
any other reason,

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.340
to email me at pfeffer@stanford.edu.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.440
And so with that, we'll launch
into the content of the webinar,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.140
which is about the book,
which is on the one hand controversial,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.340
on the other hand,
some of it is complete common sense.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.870
It is a book that is written based
upon the following observation.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.090
And the observation is on the one
hand we have an enormous,

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.280
a huge, a gigantic industry of leadership.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.310
For instance, there are more than 2.6
million entries on Google Scholar

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.150
such as just for the scholarly literature.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.646
140 million results from a Google Search.

00:02:36.646 --> 00:02:42.326
117,000 entries on Amazon.com
the estimates are McKinsey estimates

00:02:42.326 --> 00:02:48.040
$14 billion spent annually on
training just in the United States.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:55.240
I estimate using AFTB data
about 20 billion or above.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:01.630
Robert Kennedy estimates $50 billion
a year, it's a big industry.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:04.710
Virtually every business school and
many other places of

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:10.040
course have leadership activity
as a common part of their thing.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.950
However, notwithstanding that we have just
spent all this money, and have done so

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.490
for lots of time,
workplaces by every single measure

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:22.670
that you can get provided by
virtually every source, are horrible.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.490
There are low levels of employee
engagement, as reported by Gallup.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:31.390
There are low levels of job satisfaction
and declining reported by Gallup,

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:35.020
by the Conference Board,
by Right Management and by Mercer.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.800
You can look at the data yourself.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.150
No wonder.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.660
My favorite statistic in Parade magazine,

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:46.270
which used to be part of the Sunday
supplement in most Newspapers did a survey

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.770
in 2012 and found that 35% of US employees
said they would willingly forego

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.020
a substantial pay raise if their
direct supervisor would get fired.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.325
So that tells you something about
the state of the workplace in spite of all

00:04:00.325 --> 00:04:01.315
this leadership training.

00:04:01.315 --> 00:04:05.815
And by the way, it is important to
recognize that the Gallup studies and

00:04:05.815 --> 00:04:08.385
the other studies I have cited,
not this parade thing but

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:12.530
everything else, deals for
the most part with worldwide surveys.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.940
So, this is not just a US problem.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.940
This is a problem around the world,

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.690
lots of investment in leadership,
bad workplaces.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:25.249
By the way, things aren't so
good for the leaders, either.

00:04:25.249 --> 00:04:29.210
Leaders are losing their jobs.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.010
CEO tenures are declining, have been for
a while, it's documented by

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:38.350
the Conference Board and consulting
firm lose, but it's worse than that.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:45.080
A Stanford alumni group recently got
together in Washington DC to discuss my

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.650
book, and one of the leading polling
questions was, have you been fired?

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:54.270
And these are now Stanford people,
highly selective, highly motivated,

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.730
highly intelligent.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.770
Certainly not a random
sample 60% of them said yes.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:05.220
So it is not just an issue of you're
an Uber driver losing your job or

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.950
it's not just an issue there's
turn over at the top but there is.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:12.940
These are people throughout the
organization who are losing their jobs in

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:17.950
spite of the fact that they have MBAs and
other forms of business education.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.270
Next measure.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.220
So we've got leaders using their job,

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:26.920
we've got workplaces are horrible
Face of all this big spin.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.100
Third thing is go after companies.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.580
How well are your leadership
development efforts going?

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.060
Are you getting good leaders?

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.120
Are you producing sufficient leaders?

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.240
And you have to understand that
the people that you're asking are biased.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.951
To give you a positive answer because
they've actually spent the money.

00:05:44.951 --> 00:05:47.740
Only 7% of senior managers polled by a UK

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.560
business school thought their firms
effectively developed global leaders.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.525
An Accenture survey found that only 8%
of executives felt their company was

00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:56.100
effective in developing leaders.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:01.000
The answers for corporate productivity
reported that even among the best,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.810
highest performing companies,

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.890
66 reported that they were ineffective at
developing leaders and were getting worse.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:10.960
Bill Gentry at the Center for Creative
Leadership who has a new book out by

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.310
the way which I highly recommend, who
summarized numerous research studies noted

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.238
that one-half of all leaders and managers
are ineffective in their current role.

00:06:19.238 --> 00:06:23.690
Leaders are failing,

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:28.780
as you know there's from 1999 to
2005 about 40% of companies and

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.940
their leaders got in trouble enough
to warrant major press attention.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.030
Many Americans believe
there's a leadership crisis,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.880
DDI and other global HR consulting firms,
that reported from a survey that one-third

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.830
did not consider their
own manager effective.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.340
And their global leadership forecast,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.920
noted that by their own admission leaders
are falling short, but leaders consistent

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:54.160
in the hallmarks of the quality of
the leadership in their own organization.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.100
So my conclusion, based upon this is
that the leadership industry has failed,

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.440
as it continues to do what it is
doing it will continue to fail.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:07.240
So then the question is what are the
causes and what might we do about it.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.245
The first cause is that if you,

00:07:09.245 --> 00:07:12.910
anybody listening to this call today can
go out and become a leadership expert,

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:16.370
there are no requirements you can't
go out today and do back surgery.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.370
And you can't go out today and

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.850
build a bridge or fly an airplane, but
you can in fact be a leadership expert

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:25.690
of Ink magazines list of the top
leadership experts published in 2014.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:30.710
Only four had a doctorate in a relevant
field one had no degree at all.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:35.340
Two had degrees in divinity which I think
is actually important the leadership

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.510
teaching is a lot around lay preaching.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.490
Five did not have any degrees in business.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:45.717
As you go to the website which I did,
the expertise that the exports

00:07:45.717 --> 00:07:50.174
are telling you is talking so
that they're great at talking whether or

00:07:50.174 --> 00:07:53.371
not they're great at
doing anything certainly.

00:07:53.371 --> 00:07:55.455
But I had a back surgery
a couple years ago,

00:07:55.455 --> 00:07:58.771
when you get in a plane the number
one requirement for the pilot or

00:07:58.771 --> 00:08:01.364
your surgeon is not
the ability I could describe.

00:08:01.364 --> 00:08:05.754
Even I by the way could describe how to do
surgery, you wouldn't want me doing it.

00:08:05.754 --> 00:08:10.445
So we do have a situation in which many
people are delivering material with no

00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:11.630
qualifications.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.730
And then they are able to do that because,
of course, the evaluation of most

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.859
of these leadership programs,
well some aren't evaluated at all.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:26.470
Some that are evaluated or
evaluated by the expenditure of resources.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.090
How many people want into the program?

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:29.110
How many hours?

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.760
Okay, that's fine,

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.172
some of them then are evaluated by what
do the people think of the program?

00:08:34.172 --> 00:08:37.890
And these are sometimes
called smiley-faced sheets or

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:42.430
happy sheets where we ask people,
did you have a good experience?

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:48.385
Now the problem with a self rated
experience is that there is another field,

00:08:48.385 --> 00:08:52.655
it's called education and teaching,
in which we know a couple of things.

00:08:52.655 --> 00:08:57.295
The first thing we know is that student
ratings are correlated zero with

00:08:57.295 --> 00:09:01.097
the objective amount of
material that students learned.

00:09:01.097 --> 00:09:06.653
And secondly, we know that teacher
ratings are highly predicted

00:09:06.653 --> 00:09:12.812
by 30 second silent videos rated
by other people of the teachers.

00:09:12.812 --> 00:09:17.510
So you say, how can a 30 second silent
video of people who will never see this

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:21.150
person again, predict their teaching
ratings at the end of the course and

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.210
the answer is of course, that they
are both picking up on the same thing.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.680
Which is some measure of personality,

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.480
social dominance, affability kind
of commanding the room, whatever.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:38.261
None of which by the way, are necessarily
what we have been talking about which

00:09:38.261 --> 00:09:41.529
is employee engagement,
success of leaders, etc.

00:09:41.529 --> 00:09:44.202
So in the field of leadership development,

00:09:44.202 --> 00:09:49.600
you reward, you get what you reward as
we've learned from the quality movement.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.490
In the case of this you reward
entertainment, you get entertainment.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:59.280
Few I know of none, companies assess
their interventions in their leadership

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.230
space by using measures that assess
the effects of leaders on their workplace

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:08.323
as such by employee engagement,
job satisfaction, turnover, etc.

00:10:08.323 --> 00:10:13.240
Next, we're going to come back
to this divergent interests,

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.750
between the leaders and those led, and

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.790
even differences in measures of leader
well being and organizational success.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.940
So when I was writing this book,

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.930
I gave a copy of the manuscript
to a friend of mine.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.750
Very knowledgeable human being who's
been senior partner at Accenture, and

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:33.320
is now president of Menlo College
in Menlo Park California.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.550
And has served on a number
of corporate boards.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:36.990
He read the book,
he gave me some comments.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.426
He said, by the way based on your book,
what should I do to be a better leader?

00:10:39.426 --> 00:10:43.010
And I said,
that's an interesting question.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.060
What do you mean by better leader?

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:49.960
I said, when Stan O'Neal drove
Merrill Lynch into the ground in

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.210
the 2007 financial crisis,
he left with $140 million.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.490
Just your standard Mrs.
O'Neil, he did a pretty good job.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:00.940
If you're one of the Merrill Lynch
people in the residue left behind,

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:02.660
he didn't do such a good job.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.460
And there are many examples like that.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:08.840
So I think what that example
illustrates is that we are way,

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:14.500
way, way, way too sloppy in thinking
about leader effectiveness.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:17.080
So you tell me,
is this leader effective or not?

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.300
We have to specify the criteria.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:21.960
Are we talking about work unit criteria?

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.120
Are we talking about
organizational level criteria?

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:26.970
Over what time period?

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:32.150
And even if we measure profit
margins versus profit growth,

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.770
depending upon how you measure
the margins of negative growth,

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.620
those things aren't
going to be correlated,

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:40.600
let alone be correlated with how
the leader has done for him or herself.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:45.460
So you need to be I think very precise
about how we measure leader effectiveness.

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:47.500
And also recognize that what's good for

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.300
the organization is not good for
the leader.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:54.160
You see companies layoff people all
the time if it helps their profits, but

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.120
it doesn't help the people laid off.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:01.040
You see leaders leaving in
failed circumstances with huge

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.950
severance packages, even though
they destroyed the organization.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.770
So there is a divergence of interest
that we need to pay attention to.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:14.300
There's also an enormous
amount of conceptual

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:19.610
imprecision about leadership ideas, and
this is not just an academic issue.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:23.970
So if I said we're going to improve
sanitary practices in medicine, which have

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.890
been a big problem, on which the Institute
of Medicines some years ago in a report

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:32.860
called Keeping Patients Safe, said we are
killing about 80 or 100,000 people a year.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:35.778
In fact, we're going to improve
sanitary practices, we would say,

00:12:35.778 --> 00:12:40.430
what are the sanitary practices,
how would we measure their occurrence?

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.800
So that we can actually then go and see.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.790
How much of it are being done.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.698
And if we had used interventions,
we could measure improvement.

00:12:48.698 --> 00:12:51.716
If I say, we are going to have
more authentic leadership,

00:12:51.716 --> 00:12:53.400
we've got a big problem.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:58.120
Because as I've gone on the web there's no
definition, there's no precision about or

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.190
consensus about how to measure
authentic leadership or

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.750
decide whether or
not it's a good thing or a bad thing.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.850
Nobody knows how to measure it.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:09.140
If there's not consensus on measurement,
if we don't know what hand washing means,

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:13.190
in the case of medicine,
we cannot improve it.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:14.480
And that's the case here.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:20.430
So conceptual in precision, and we talk
about these things in very sloppy ways,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:24.525
makes it hard to focus both
programs to make it better, and

00:13:24.525 --> 00:13:26.780
assessments of whether or
not those programs have worked.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.715
This is simple common sense.

00:13:29.715 --> 00:13:32.344
So there are some remedies
obviously suggested

00:13:32.344 --> 00:13:35.110
implicitly in what we've
talked about thus far.

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:36.900
One, we ought to measure outcomes.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.690
I mean, any leadership program that only
gives out smiley face sheets I don't think

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.390
is doing the right thing,
because you got to measure outcome.

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:46.290
Over some time period,
I understand it's difficult,

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.980
but you need to measure satisfaction,
engagement, bullying,

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:54.970
turnover the number of potential
successors, leadership,

00:13:54.970 --> 00:14:00.134
success in their jobs, do the people stay,

00:14:00.134 --> 00:14:06.460
do the people being led like the leaders,
a bunch of things like that.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.470
And, of course, we need to select
scientists not entertainers

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:14.150
to do the work of leadership evaluation
and I would argue leadership development.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:18.810
We need to acknowledge
the conflicts of objectives and

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:22.610
then work to understand where they come
from and how they might be reduced.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:25.350
Instead of just laughing
at the story of Stan O'Neil

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:29.520
leaving with a $140 million as
Merrill Lynch goes into the toilet.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:33.210
Or Carly Fiorina leaving with $50
million and then going to run for

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:36.600
president on her business record,
her pretty spotty business record.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.420
We need to understand
how this is possible.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:43.990
Now banking, after the financial crisis,
there has been more attention to this and

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:45.830
they've got this idea of claw backs.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.620
So if I make a lot of money
under false pretenses and

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:55.285
then the company falls apart I get to try
to get you to repay some of that money.

00:14:55.285 --> 00:14:58.145
But this is not a talk on
incentive alignment, but

00:14:58.145 --> 00:15:01.165
incentives I think are really critical.

00:15:01.165 --> 00:15:05.145
And in many instances
the incentives are not aligned.

00:15:05.145 --> 00:15:10.035
The senior executive incentives
are very often, way too short-term and

00:15:10.035 --> 00:15:14.285
way too focused on things
that can be gained,

00:15:14.285 --> 00:15:17.760
including obviously stock price,
which is gained all the time.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.790
If you don't believe that,
just go back and

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:22.410
look at the history of
the thing with Enron.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:28.180
I mean they gave people stock
options that paid off, so the stock

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:31.220
got to a high price, unfortunately for
a very,very short period of time.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:35.600
So we need to do something to
align incentives way better.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.290
Here's another problem
that I think we all face.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:43.520
So the number one word,
probably not the number one word,

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:44.670
it's one of the common words.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:50.711
The number one word that is used about
me and my books and my Fortune blogs,

00:15:50.711 --> 00:15:56.077
I do blogs on Fortune.com,
I have a column about twice a month.

00:15:56.077 --> 00:16:00.960
Is that it's dark, or
that it's depressing, or

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.994
that it's I don't know what.

00:16:03.994 --> 00:16:09.046
So the opposite of dark or
depressing, of course, is inspire!

00:16:09.046 --> 00:16:13.037
And the leadership world
loves inspiration.

00:16:13.037 --> 00:16:15.790
So I gave a talk at
the Vlerick Business School.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:20.030
I was going to use an example from
that talk in my book, and so I said,

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:22.290
I better make sure I
spell Vlerick correctly.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.101
So I went to the Vlerick Business School,

00:16:24.101 --> 00:16:26.861
which is a very good business
school in Belgium's, website.

00:16:26.861 --> 00:16:28.962
And the first thing on their website is,
looking for

00:16:28.962 --> 00:16:30.359
an inspiring management course.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.830
Australian Graduate School of Management's
website notes that the school creates

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:36.910
inspirational learning opportunities.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:41.666
I've spent some time on some websites
of engineering schools, law schools,

00:16:41.666 --> 00:16:42.828
medical schools.

00:16:42.828 --> 00:16:47.079
They will talk about material
that is professionally relevant,

00:16:47.079 --> 00:16:51.020
that is scientifically rigorous,
that is well delivered,

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:55.211
that it is available online into
various learning modalities.

00:16:55.211 --> 00:16:59.021
But I don't see many people going
to medical school to be inspired.

00:16:59.021 --> 00:17:00.711
[LAUGH] Or to law school to be inspired.

00:17:00.711 --> 00:17:02.571
They're going to medical school and

00:17:02.571 --> 00:17:05.271
law school to be educated
in professionally relevant

00:17:05.271 --> 00:17:08.817
knowledge that they are then going to
be able to use in a substantive way.

00:17:08.817 --> 00:17:12.954
And so I think our search for inspiration
is not only kind of a joke, but

00:17:12.954 --> 00:17:17.739
it is extremely harmful, and by the way
this goes on inside of companies as well.

00:17:17.739 --> 00:17:22.023
First of all, it turns leadership and
leadership writing and speaking and

00:17:22.023 --> 00:17:27.110
whatever into a form of lay preaching
because now we're into inspiring people.

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:29.869
If you were to read
Sigmund Freud's Delusion and Dream and

00:17:29.869 --> 00:17:33.416
compare his discussion of the origins and
functions of religion to those of

00:17:33.416 --> 00:17:37.097
the leadership industry, the parallels
are, of course, quite striking.

00:17:37.097 --> 00:17:41.110
And the real problem with inspiration,
I want to be inspired.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:44.930
And so if I wanted to be inspired
in medicine, I would take,

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:47.948
well, most people die of
pancreatic cancer in a year, and

00:17:47.948 --> 00:17:49.870
the five-year survival
rate is close to zero.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:54.990
But somebody, I'm sure there is somebody
who would have survived by, and

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:57.450
this distribution,
there's always somebody at the tail.

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:01.680
So let's find this wonderful inspiring
person, put them in front of an audience,

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.380
and give an inspirational talk
about surviving pancreatic cancer,

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:08.500
which by the way will do nothing to
improve the finding of a cure for cancer,

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:12.830
the treatment of cancer for anybody in
the audience, but it's kind of inspiring.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:18.710
So the search for inspiration drives
the use of rare and unusual examples.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:20.630
And you see this all the time.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.304
So one of my good friends who's
endorsed this book, Jim Collins,

00:18:23.304 --> 00:18:25.530
wrote a book called Good to Great.

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.144
You read Good to Great.

00:18:27.144 --> 00:18:31.290
He started off with 1400 companies,
as I recall, somewhere between 10 and 15,

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.490
maybe 11 or 14 made the transition.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:38.350
So if you were a betting man,
you would not make that bet.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.210
I mean, rare and
unusual examples are interesting, but

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:43.314
they don't really inform a lot
about what we should do.

00:18:43.314 --> 00:18:49.230
And secondly, the search for inspiration
leads to the reconstruction and

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:54.400
misremembering of events to produce
an uplifting inspiring narrative.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:59.400
The book which was made into a movie, Lone
Survivor, of the poor guy who survived

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:04.390
out of the 16 Marines dumped
into Afghanistan turned

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:07.740
out to be quite inspiring, also turned
out that the more they looked at it and

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:11.360
talked to other people,
turned out to not be completely true.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.440
Inspiration, that's another problem.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:18.950
It is a horrible way to accomplish change.

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:21.690
It sets unrealistic expectations for
ourselves and

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:25.170
others, it raises motivation,
but only for a short time.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:28.779
Many of the inspirational stories you
hear, I would say all of them, so

00:19:28.779 --> 00:19:32.090
when you hear some inspirational
story about some fabulous CEO,

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:34.316
do about 30 minutes of due diligence, and

00:19:34.316 --> 00:19:38.073
my bet would be you'll find a good
portion of what you heard is incorrect.

00:19:38.073 --> 00:19:41.430
And by the way, we know how to
accomplish behavioral change.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.960
If you have a drug problem,
a smoking problem,

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:48.020
an eating problem,
a problem with alcohol, all of these

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:54.870
interventions to change your behavior
with respect to food, drink or

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:59.830
illegal drugs, or for that matter, legal
drugs, all understand several principles.

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:05.620
Number one, your behavior is a function of
the social environment in which you are.

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:08.280
So you're much more likely to drink
if you're surrounded by drinkers,

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.790
much more likely to smoke if
you're surrounded by smokers,

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:14.380
much more likely to gamble if you're
surrounded by gamblers, you get the point.

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:17.240
And therefore, one of the first things
these intervention programs do is,

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.510
I'm going to separate you from the people
engaging in the behavior that we

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:21.770
want you to stop.

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:24.940
So we need to worry about that.

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:28.070
Secondly, we understand
the importance of measurement.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:32.142
In the words of the quality movement,
that which gets measured gets done.

00:20:32.142 --> 00:20:34.530
So that goes back to the measurement
thing we talked about.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:39.300
We also know the importance
of visual priming.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.496
It's how casinos and retail stores work.

00:20:42.496 --> 00:20:46.013
It is why Brian Wansink, the famous
guy who has a book about dieting,

00:20:46.013 --> 00:20:50.018
says that the best way to lose weight is
serve your portions on smaller plates,

00:20:50.018 --> 00:20:52.159
because large plates cue you to eat more.

00:20:52.159 --> 00:20:55.519
[COUGH] So instead of worrying
about inspiring people,

00:20:55.519 --> 00:21:00.266
if we worried about measuring priming,
giving people reminders of what they

00:21:00.266 --> 00:21:04.520
should do and not do, we would be in much,
much, much better shape.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:10.939
So, the recommendations that follow from
this is to do due diligence on leaders, so

00:21:10.939 --> 00:21:13.005
that you know that the stories are true.

00:21:13.005 --> 00:21:16.441
Stop chasing inspiration,
you need facts, not fables.

00:21:19.274 --> 00:21:23.875
And understand the pitfalls of
trying to learn from rare events and

00:21:23.875 --> 00:21:25.280
extreme examples.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.280
They can be instructive, but

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:33.060
you will see that the great 14 people,
14 companies make the transition.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:34.980
To me the interesting question is,

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:41.230
what about the common sense things that
Collins recommends is so difficult to do.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:43.770
And how could you make them easier?

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:51.200
Okay, next we're going to talk about
trade-offs, ends versus means.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:59.329
Everybody wants to do great things,
but they want to do it in nice ways.

00:21:59.329 --> 00:22:03.702
New York Times, June 15th, 2014.

00:22:06.209 --> 00:22:11.850
Where Dishonesty is Best Policy,
US Soccer Falls Short.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:15.770
Turns out you want to win soccer games,
you gotta be able to fake injuries.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:23.122
I just watched the match between
Rio Real Madrid and Atletico.

00:22:23.122 --> 00:22:25.441
And you can see them,
somebody touches somebody, aah,

00:22:25.441 --> 00:22:27.320
they fall on the floor or whatever.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:31.930
Anyway, the article asks the question, are
Americans bad at playacting, and if so,

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:33.270
should they try to get better.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:38.241
Gamesmanship and embellishment
are part of high-level soccer.

00:22:38.241 --> 00:22:39.994
Players exaggerate contact.

00:22:39.994 --> 00:22:43.350
They turn niggling knocks into
something closer to grim death.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:46.090
You can add, by the way,
take that and substitute a few words.

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:47.781
Are Americans bad at playacting?

00:22:47.781 --> 00:22:49.420
If so, should they try to get better?

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.920
Gamesmanship and embellishment are part
of not only of high-level soccer,

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:54.980
they're part of organizational life.

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:57.225
People exaggerate
the amount of work they do.

00:22:57.225 --> 00:23:04.680
They turn middling projects into
things that are huge accomplishments.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.760
So an interesting issue.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.800
Many of the most celebrated
leaders engage in behaviors and

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:16.530
use strategies that I'm willing to bet
are completely outside of almost what

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.770
virtually every leadership
development program features.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:22.070
So everybody who says, I want to be
a great leader like Abraham Lincoln, but

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:24.970
nobody's going to teach you
to be like Abraham Lincoln.

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:29.732
You go watch Lincoln,
the fabulous movie, what did Lincoln do?

00:23:29.732 --> 00:23:33.237
Lincoln lied about his intention to
free the slaves at the start of his

00:23:33.237 --> 00:23:34.720
political career.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:37.860
Lincoln lied about where the Southern
delegation was when he was trying to

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:40.780
end the war.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:43.990
Lincoln, in order to pass the 13th
Amendment that outlawed slavery,

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:48.580
was willing to basically go down to
the floor, well, send his emissaries

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:52.970
into Congress and trade positions,
basically sell positions for votes.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:56.812
Now this is like in any leadership
program you want to read about.

00:23:56.812 --> 00:24:01.808
Nelson Mandela, completely inconsistent,
one day a raging communist and

00:24:01.808 --> 00:24:07.518
a proposer of violence, and sometime later
a peace person interested in capitalism.

00:24:07.518 --> 00:24:08.710
You can go down the list.

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:12.835
All of these people were willing,
because they were so

00:24:12.835 --> 00:24:19.121
committed to accomplishing something,
they were willing to do whatever it took.

00:24:19.121 --> 00:24:21.768
So if I have to tell you a little story.

00:24:21.768 --> 00:24:24.578
If I have to not tell you
what I'm really going to do.

00:24:24.578 --> 00:24:30.718
If I have to, say one thing one day and
a different thing the other, I'm willing

00:24:30.718 --> 00:24:36.879
to do that in order to accomplish this
major change that I'm trying to get done.

00:24:36.879 --> 00:24:39.640
This is a wonderful thing which I
would urge you to read on your own.

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:42.797
It's Why Machiavelli Still Matters,
from an article,

00:24:42.797 --> 00:24:46.559
an opinion piece in the New York Times,
December 9th, 2013.

00:24:46.559 --> 00:24:51.605
I would highlight only one sentence,
which is Machiavelli teaches that

00:24:51.605 --> 00:24:56.910
in a world where so many are not good,
you must learn to be able to not be good.

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.986
And then the proper aim of a leader
is to maintain his state and

00:25:00.986 --> 00:25:02.336
not incidentally, his job.

00:25:02.336 --> 00:25:06.893
There are never easy choices, and
prudence consists of knowing how to

00:25:06.893 --> 00:25:10.681
recognize the qualities of
the hard decisions you face and

00:25:10.681 --> 00:25:13.710
choosing the less bad as
what is the most good.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:17.750
So this, the Kumbaya nature,
of the leadership industry and

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:23.191
the Kumbaya which I just find now not only
offensive, but completely unhelpful for

00:25:23.191 --> 00:25:27.660
what we are trying to accomplish
in making leaders effective.

00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:31.374
I just find it distressing and
not helpful.

00:25:31.374 --> 00:25:35.642
Because, like here, we have students
from Stanford, believe it or

00:25:35.642 --> 00:25:40.000
not, go out into the world,
I believe in openness and transparency.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:40.710
Not a problem.

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:41.565
What happened?

00:25:41.565 --> 00:25:44.710
Well, I'm on the phone with you
because I'm about to get fired.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.777
Why?
I told my boss what I thought of her.

00:25:46.777 --> 00:25:49.096
[LAUGH] Not a good thing to do.

00:25:49.096 --> 00:25:51.083
[LAUGH] Unless your
opinion is pretty high.

00:25:51.083 --> 00:25:55.128
So I mean, we're sending people out and
they are losing their jobs or

00:25:55.128 --> 00:25:57.172
they are having career setbacks.

00:25:57.172 --> 00:26:01.375
And I find that just really halucious,
but whatever.

00:26:01.375 --> 00:26:04.526
Perhaps the biggest problem of
all in this leadership role,

00:26:04.526 --> 00:26:07.259
is the confusion between
what should be and what is.

00:26:07.259 --> 00:26:11.145
In the leadership domain we have a big
discrepancy with how we believe leaders

00:26:11.145 --> 00:26:14.551
ought to be, leaders ought to be
honest and open and transparent and

00:26:14.551 --> 00:26:17.386
tell the truth and look out for
the well being of others.

00:26:17.386 --> 00:26:21.287
And, by the way, there's a lot of research
that suggests if leaders did that,

00:26:21.287 --> 00:26:24.607
the workplaces would be better,
that employees would be happier,

00:26:24.607 --> 00:26:26.970
their engagement levels would be higher.

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:29.560
People wouldn't be saying
on the parade survey that

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:34.290
I'd give up a substantial raise
to get rid of my boss, etc., etc.

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:38.570
And then we need to look at how leaders
actually are, understand that discrepancy,

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:40.150
and understand why it exists.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:45.626
And I'm going to give you just a couple of
examples, but these can be fixed as well.

00:26:45.626 --> 00:26:50.387
And the two questions to ask about the
oft-touted leadership attributes is there

00:26:50.387 --> 00:26:54.596
any evidence that most or many leaders
display the desired behaviors and

00:26:54.596 --> 00:26:57.842
since they don't,
what is the logic for the opposite?

00:26:57.842 --> 00:26:58.544
And by the way,

00:26:58.544 --> 00:27:01.846
you can play this game out as you look
at the current political campaign.

00:27:01.846 --> 00:27:03.898
Since the leading candidate for

00:27:03.898 --> 00:27:09.068
the most part bear almost no resemblance
to what we claim a leader should be or do.

00:27:09.068 --> 00:27:10.650
One example is modesty.

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.652
Here we go, that is in fact the chapter
in a book written more than two and

00:27:14.652 --> 00:27:17.346
a half years ago the name
Donald Trump appears.

00:27:17.346 --> 00:27:21.666
So Jim Collins does a great highlight
of Level 5 leaders who were

00:27:21.666 --> 00:27:26.460
characterized as being both driven but
also incredibly modest.

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:30.220
So you didn't see their names all
over the news, media, and whatever.

00:27:30.220 --> 00:27:32.090
Modesty is, of course, a virtue.

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:36.280
And most importantly,
modest leaders share credit with others.

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:40.150
And in sharing a credit with other people
is more likely to attract other people,

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:45.760
because people are more likely to work on
a project for which they get some credit.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:49.936
Secondly, people are more
likely to work on a project for

00:27:49.936 --> 00:27:52.555
which other people listen to them.

00:27:52.555 --> 00:27:57.510
And thirdly, when people give you credit,
you feel better about yourself.

00:27:57.510 --> 00:28:00.736
So modesty is a good thing to do.

00:28:00.736 --> 00:28:05.201
But in a book called The Productive
Narcissist written by Michael Maccoby,

00:28:05.201 --> 00:28:10.015
he noted that many of the most venerated
business leaders exhibited high levels of

00:28:10.015 --> 00:28:11.210
narcissism.

00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:14.950
Steve Jobs, Jack Welch, Larry Ellison,
Donald Trump in a book that was written 20

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:19.970
years ago, so the person doesn't change,
Michael Eisner, and a bunch of others.

00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:24.280
And we know, from research on narcissism,
that many, many, many studies, and

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:29.860
you can look this up on scholar.google.com
which accesses the scholarly literature.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:34.375
And look at the effects of, if you put in
a phrase, the effects of narcissism, and

00:28:34.375 --> 00:28:38.697
you'll show, so what you'll find is
that many studies show that immodesty,

00:28:38.697 --> 00:28:43.218
self-promotion, self-aggrandizement,
and unwarranted self-confidence

00:28:43.218 --> 00:28:47.233
reliably helps people obtain leadership
positions in the first place.

00:28:47.233 --> 00:28:51.521
Then once in them, affects their
ability to hold on to those positions,

00:28:51.521 --> 00:28:56.161
get higher pay, and even helps in some
dimensions, though not all of group and

00:28:56.161 --> 00:28:58.082
organizational performance.

00:28:58.082 --> 00:29:01.187
And so if we want modest leaders
we need to understand, number one,

00:29:01.187 --> 00:29:03.520
why are we selecting for the opposite?

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:07.970
And number two,
what about immodesty is helping people and

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:13.399
how are there other ways to accomplish
that or build organizations where

00:29:13.399 --> 00:29:18.564
these qualities don't have the same
positive effects that they currently do?

00:29:18.564 --> 00:29:21.070
Second example would be authenticity.

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:23.820
There's a big authentic
leadership movement

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:27.530
represented by Bill George's book,
True North, written with my colleague and

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:29.700
former MBA student, Pete Sims.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:34.066
The idea is that people ought to be
true to their real inner thoughts and

00:29:34.066 --> 00:29:38.813
feelings, to their inner character,
that you need to be true to yourself.

00:29:38.813 --> 00:29:42.031
And that if you're not true to
yourself and put on a shell or

00:29:42.031 --> 00:29:46.040
put on an act, people can see
through attempts at deception.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:52.049
And, by the way, people prefer to
relate to people who are authentic.

00:29:52.049 --> 00:29:55.210
Great leaders are great actors.

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:55.900
And, by the way,

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:59.770
if you believe you can see through people
who are putting on a show, good luck.

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:01.730
There's been a bunch of
empirical literature on this.

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:05.630
People are very bad at uncovering lies,
even when they're told to do so

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:09.300
and they're told that some of
the people talking to them are lying.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.870
And all you need to do is look at
the Bernie Madoff financial scandal or

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:13.800
many of these other things.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:18.040
We believe what we want to believe and
we're quite bad at uncovering deception.

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:22.773
Authenticity itself may be impossible
because it says be true to your true self

00:30:22.773 --> 00:30:24.433
but what true self is that?

00:30:24.433 --> 00:30:26.359
Is it true to your managerial self?

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:30.690
Yourself as a parent, yourself as a child?

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.590
Yourself in various relationships?

00:30:34.590 --> 00:30:38.174
We occupy in our lives numerous roles.

00:30:38.174 --> 00:30:43.093
And in each one of these roles we have
to do and be slightly different things.

00:30:43.093 --> 00:30:46.976
And authenticity is about being
true to your inner self but

00:30:46.976 --> 00:30:49.828
leaders need to be true not to themselves,

00:30:49.828 --> 00:30:55.070
but to what the people in their work
setting want and need from them.

00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:58.087
So when Gary Loveman,
who at the time was running Harrah's,

00:30:58.087 --> 00:31:01.463
which then became Caesars the big
casino company, came to my class.

00:31:01.463 --> 00:31:06.046
And for the first time in 2004,
after I had written a case on him,

00:31:06.046 --> 00:31:08.190
he arrived, he did a great job.

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:11.956
It was only at lunch that I learned
that Gary Loveman had the flu and

00:31:11.956 --> 00:31:15.330
was running a temperature
of 101 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:20.701
Now, as a sentient human being,
the last thing Gary Loveman wants to do,

00:31:20.701 --> 00:31:25.638
if he's being true to himself,
is show up in my class sick as a dog and

00:31:25.638 --> 00:31:30.602
spend two sessions talking to these
students who he doesn't know.

00:31:30.602 --> 00:31:34.533
But he understands that part of his
deal is he's agreed to do this,

00:31:34.533 --> 00:31:36.249
I'd have let him off the hook.

00:31:36.249 --> 00:31:39.344
But part of the deal is
he has the capacity to,

00:31:39.344 --> 00:31:41.879
I feel like hell, doesn't matter.

00:31:41.879 --> 00:31:46.606
I'm with employees who will see me
once a year, I have to look energized,

00:31:46.606 --> 00:31:48.400
engaged, concerned.

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:53.535
Even if my daughter's giving me trouble,
even if there are marital issues,

00:31:53.535 --> 00:31:58.512
even if I'm tired beyond comprehension,
leaders need to be authentic and

00:31:58.512 --> 00:32:02.473
true to what the people around
them want and need from them.

00:32:02.473 --> 00:32:05.855
My second example would
be Allison Davis-Blake.

00:32:05.855 --> 00:32:09.559
I like to say I knew Allison Davis-Blake
before she was hyphenated.

00:32:09.559 --> 00:32:16.470
When Alison Davis was a doctoral student
at the Stanford Business School,

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:21.187
she was smart, nice,
decent, the most painfully

00:32:21.187 --> 00:32:26.630
shy human being I have ever
encountered in the world.

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:29.820
If she spoke in class which she almost
never did, you couldn't hear her.

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:35.860
Allison Davis-Blake is stepping down this
year as the Dean of the Ross School of

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:39.080
the University of Michigan, one of
the leading business schools in the world.

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:42.940
She told me a couple weeks ago
that she is a finalist one of two,

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:48.130
for a provost position in one of the top
50 universities in the United States.

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:52.360
You do not get from where
she was to where she is

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:56.870
by being the shyest person in the room,
some person that no one can hear.

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:02.340
The idea that Alison Davis-Blake should
have been true to the original Alison,

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:08.270
who couldn't talk, who had no or exhibited
no self-confidence, is just I think nuts.

00:33:08.270 --> 00:33:10.030
People remake themselves all the time.

00:33:10.030 --> 00:33:12.870
And if you're going to want to
be successful you have to.

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:18.900
Now, let me give you some final
recommendations, and then we'll have lots

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:22.817
of time for questions, because on my
computer it says it only 10:38, and

00:33:22.817 --> 00:33:24.670
I was supposed to go to
approximately 10:40.

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:25.550
How do you like that?

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:26.750
Just on time.

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:29.290
My bosses here are always pleased.

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:32.040
Not that I should a good job, but
you should do a good job on time.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.310
That's even more important.

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:38.890
So, if you want to improve I think
if we're serious about making,

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:41.270
leadership better than
it's been in the past,

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:44.380
I think the first thing we need
to do is both understand and

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:48.292
then maybe remedy the disconnects between
what leaders say and what they do.

00:33:48.292 --> 00:33:53.110
Lots of leaders talk about doing all
kinds of things many of them don't do it.

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:58.070
You know I'm nice, I'm whatever,
they self advertise the wonderful calling

00:33:58.070 --> 00:34:01.930
that they display and then you go then
you hear pretty much the opposite.

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:07.310
We need to worry about and do something
about the disconnect between prescriptions

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:12.800
from leaders and the realty of
actual leaders behaviors and traits.

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:17.400
I think one of the big problems in
leadership today is that we tell people to

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:22.180
do things that, while they are
aspirational and great In many respects

00:34:22.180 --> 00:34:27.340
are so, discrepant with what we know
about fundamental human psychology and

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:31.100
leader behavior that we're basically,
it's like going to church.

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:34.200
We're telling you to be this this
person that nobody is going to be.

00:34:34.200 --> 00:34:38.260
And so it's kind of an interesting
disconnect that we need to remedy.

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:42.250
We need certainly a remedy to disconnect
this from leader performance and

00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:46.590
what happens to those leaders, the fact
that many of them have no consequences for

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:49.440
their bad behavior,
which goes on all the time.

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:55.210
We also need to remedy the disconnect
between what most people think to want

00:34:55.210 --> 00:35:00.330
which is uplifting stories about
relatively rare events and

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:01.980
what I think most people need.

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:04.530
You know one of the reasons why medicine.

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:07.040
I believe has made much more
progress than management.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:09.170
Is that medicine is much
more science based.

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:13.038
So when you show me lots of people
dying on pancreatic cancer.

00:35:13.038 --> 00:35:18.210
I'm not going to find the one that doesn't
make this person into some role model.

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:20.060
I'm not going to say, wow!

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:23.050
The death rate for
pancreatic cancer I find depressing.

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:24.890
It's unacceptable, but not depressing.

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:27.430
It is what it is.

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:29.290
If what it is,
you are going to make it better,

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:31.680
you are going to understand why it is and
how to make it better.

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:35.070
You are going to evaluate your
intervention in terms of whether or

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:37.590
not they generate improvement.

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:41.280
This idea that you ought to be in
the inspiration business is crazy.

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:45.860
And then I'd finally impress most
importantly, we need to evaluate

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:50.860
leadership development programs by
their actual effects on human beings.

00:35:50.860 --> 00:35:55.540
On what they do, on how they do it,

00:35:55.540 --> 00:36:01.980
on their own leadership on,
you know, on employee engagement,

00:36:01.980 --> 00:36:08.310
we need to have a much more, an evaluation
system that evaluates something,

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.010
whether or not we had a nice time or
liked the [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.530
So that's the end of my
follow up presentation,

00:36:13.530 --> 00:36:16.640
it is on time by my schedule, and so

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:21.230
we will now let you ask questions or make
comments or do anything else you want.

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:25.080
I very much appreciate
the wonderful high attendance, and

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:29.740
I hope you will all take this message
extremely seriously, and not say,

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:35.150
well this is depressing, a word that
I don't really believe we should use.

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:39.930
&gt;&gt; All right, so thank you Jeffrey for
the really wonderful presentation and for

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:41.570
being on time
&gt;&gt; And

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:43.380
I will take a few minutes now to ask.

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:46.260
There have been many,
many questions that have been coming in.

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:48.900
So we'll do our best to
answer some of them.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:54.360
And so Jeffrey,
one of the questions that came in.

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:57.300
Related to this theme of gamesmanship and

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:01.140
being authentic not to yourself but
to the people around you.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:03.650
What are some ways that you
found that you can understand

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:07.580
what the others around you
are wanting you to be?

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:11.810
&gt;&gt; Well I think you need to develop
the skill of empathic understanding.

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:13.910
And that is,
number one you need to listen.

00:37:13.910 --> 00:37:18.920
And number two you need to understand
what they're what their perspective is,

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:22.120
from how they're being paid,
how they're being evaluated,

00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:25.290
what their educational background is.

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:28.020
So a combination of what are their
key performance indicators?

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:30.110
What are they trying to get done?

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:33.950
And they may not be trying to get
done what you're trying to get done.

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:38.482
So to take the simple example of my
poor Gary Loveman coming to my class,

00:37:38.482 --> 00:37:42.600
Gary Loveman understands, intuitively
because he understands the situation.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:45.470
That students in the class
expect to see him.

00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:49.920
They expect to see him and they expect to
be able to engage with him with energy and

00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:50.780
intensity.

00:37:50.780 --> 00:37:53.070
And just because he's running
a 101 degree fever and

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:56.160
doesn't feel well,
their expectations have not changed.

00:37:56.160 --> 00:38:00.300
So I think in almost
every situation we can

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:02.800
figure out how people are getting paid or
rewarded?

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:04.470
What are their professional backgrounds?

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:07.700
What do they say about what
they want out of a situation?

00:38:07.700 --> 00:38:10.440
And then try to as a leader

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:14.411
understand that as you put together
how you want to present yourself.

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:21.020
One question that came up that I found
interesting was around the theme of

00:38:21.020 --> 00:38:25.260
when we speak about training, we often
think about athletes that train and

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.720
we often,
there's some programs that use coaches or

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:30.810
athletic coaches that come
to speak about leadership.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:33.910
Athletes have a very strong
drive to become better

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:38.340
athletes how about leaders
going to these programs?

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:42.250
What are your thoughts on their own
motivation to become better leaders?

00:38:42.250 --> 00:38:46.770
&gt;&gt; That's a fabulously fantastic
question because in many instances I

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:50.285
think the individual leaders who are sent
&gt;&gt; to these programs

00:38:50.285 --> 00:38:54.865
are often see them as kind
of a checker box activity.

00:38:54.865 --> 00:38:57.755
My company is sending me, I want to go.

00:38:57.755 --> 00:38:59.445
It's better then working.

00:38:59.445 --> 00:39:03.155
They may send you to a nice resort, you
get good food, you're here with somebody

00:39:03.155 --> 00:39:07.565
who doesn't challenge you too much and
it's pretty entertaining.

00:39:07.565 --> 00:39:10.530
I think the difference between an athlete.

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:15.740
By the way there's another difference.

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:20.245
The athlete's come to specifically,

00:39:20.245 --> 00:39:26.740
develop a very, very, very,
very specific and measurable skill.

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:31.570
Whether it's throwing a football or
jumping or swimming, or, running.

00:39:31.570 --> 00:39:33.290
Running a certain distance.

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:35.550
Running at a certain speed.

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:38.620
And evaluation of the coaching is done.

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:39.940
So I got a coach,

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:45.810
at the end of the day I'm no better
doing whatever I want for the coach,

00:39:45.810 --> 00:39:47.420
for the coach has failed no matter how
entertaining and inspiring they had been.

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:51.760
So the nice analogy with the athlete for
me, is not only whether or

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:56.480
not the athlete is self motivated, but
also whether or not the coaching or

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:59.220
the training is very behaviorally focused.

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:02.380
And in athletes it is, and
in leadership it often is not.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:06.561
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
That's really interesting, and

00:40:06.561 --> 00:40:10.651
kind of ties to your sense of
medicine as a scientific approach,

00:40:10.651 --> 00:40:12.166
where leadership is not.

00:40:12.166 --> 00:40:16.712
Do you have any examples of places
where this measurement of leadership

00:40:16.712 --> 00:40:19.840
achievement or
success has been successful?

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:22.310
Where an organization has been
successful at doing that?

00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:24.680
&gt;&gt; Sure, there are not many but
there are a few.

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:27.710
I think DaVita, which is a kidney dialysis

00:40:27.710 --> 00:40:33.060
company now called DaVita Healthcare
could be expanded into the primary

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:37.300
health are is a place where their
operating culture is no brag, just facts.

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:40.900
And so
they are an extremely fact based place.

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:45.340
I can recall their CEO telling me a story
about somebody he hired to be in charge of

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:48.470
their Red Woods program, which is
the leadership development program.

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:54.170
And they have people who are nurses and

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:57.170
who have worked up through
the ranks of the nurse manager.

00:40:57.170 --> 00:40:59.370
And they have people who come in as MBAs.

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:02.500
And he noticed that the people in
the program were mostly the MBAs,

00:41:02.500 --> 00:41:04.950
and he said to the guy
running the program.

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:09.920
You need to have equal
opportunity in this program for

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:14.430
the nurses because we want a balanced
set of leaders between the nurses, and

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:16.970
the people with business school degrees.

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:19.700
And about six month later he went back and

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:23.920
sat down with the guy and
he said have you fixed the problem?

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:26.040
The guy looked at him and he said no.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:27.490
And Camp looked at him and

00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:30.210
he said do you understand what
that means and the guy says yes.

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:31.430
He says I have to leave.

00:41:31.430 --> 00:41:34.070
Literally, one was the stories is people
don’t do it, they fire themselves.

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:40.870
So yes, I say there are companies,
I mean DaVita measures that the values,

00:41:40.870 --> 00:41:44.070
DaVita measures of employee engagement.

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:48.680
So you have to make, to make these
measures you have to take them seriously

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:51.100
And they would be one example.

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:54.000
&gt;&gt; Great.
There's a question here about women, and

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:56.430
how they can differentiate and
become better leaders.

00:41:56.430 --> 00:42:00.450
So, some of the traits that
you mentioned are perhaps

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:04.260
not very compatible with the way women
behave, or may perceive themselves.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.159
Do you have any thoughts on that?

00:42:08.020 --> 00:42:11.440
Sure, which will be quite controversial.

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:12.140
Or not.

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:16.500
I mean if you read Sheryl Sandberg's
book Lean In, I mean her basic advice is

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:22.150
do the best you can and not carry all
this psychological baggage with you.

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:25.690
When I, Allison Davis-Blake.

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:29.880
Can give you instance
after instance in which,

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:34.150
because she is a woman, and looks,
by the way, younger than her age.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:37.160
She's had all kinds of
assumptions made about her, and

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:38.660
all kinds of other stuff has gone on.

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:43.260
And Alison's response to that
has been to say, basically,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:47.430
women have to be twice as good to get
half the rewards that fortunately we are.

00:42:47.430 --> 00:42:48.900
Get over it the world is not a just and

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:50.920
fair place don't carry
the baggage with you.

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:55.050
And in fact is one of the things that made
her successful, the ability to you know.

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:59.790
She's often proactively gets justice for
herself.

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:02.270
I can tell you a story about
that if you're interested.

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:05.500
But in many instances this is just as bad.

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:10.140
Yes this happened, you know I'm
not going to take this personally.

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:13.130
I'm not going to let it
emotionally effect me.

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:14.970
I'm basically, going to let it roll off.

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:21.078
And just continue to do what I think I
need to do and be what I need to be.

00:43:21.078 --> 00:43:24.160
And not get, I'm so, men or
women, I don't care who you are.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:29.670
If you had asked him what are the critical
characteristic to be successful in life

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:34.620
let alone in the forms of life that we're
talking about, persistence and resilience.

00:43:34.620 --> 00:43:36.620
I mean you know the Venus Williams,

00:43:36.620 --> 00:43:39.900
Serena Williams they'll win
all the tennis matches.

00:43:39.900 --> 00:43:44.570
Life is tough, the higher you go
the more competitive it's going to be.

00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:46.700
You have to be willing to you know,

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:52.050
you have to be willing to in boxing terms,
to be able to take a punch.

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:54.660
You have to be able to knock down and
get back up, and

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:56.560
one of the people that if you look, and

00:43:56.560 --> 00:44:00.980
think about it who have been the most
persistent and resilient I think succeed.

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:01.630
So William Brown,

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:04.780
the most powerful African American
Politician in the United States,

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:09.530
Mayor of San Francisco twice, Speaker of
the California Assembly for 14 years.

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:11.630
Lost his first race for the Assembly.

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:15.380
Lost his first race for the Speakership.

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:18.710
You have to be able to
survive the setbacks and

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:22.690
the stuff that people throw at you.

00:44:22.690 --> 00:44:26.160
&gt;&gt; So, I mean in that you also
answered some questions that came in.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:30.500
We're just asking what are some of
the key leadership traits in your mind?

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:33.550
And perhaps those would be persistence and
resilience.

00:44:33.550 --> 00:44:36.210
Is there anything else you'd
like to add to that list?

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:39.870
&gt;&gt; I would say resilience and
persistence for sure, and

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:41.770
the ability to read other people.

00:44:41.770 --> 00:44:43.130
Not necessarily for their benefit, but

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:45.710
the ability to know where
other people are coming from.

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:47.030
That would be another.

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:51.470
A fourth which people underestimate
is the effect of energy.

00:44:51.470 --> 00:44:52.470
Emotions are contagious.

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:53.410
Energy is contagious.

00:44:53.410 --> 00:44:59.194
Everybody In this room, everybody's
listening to this podcast understands.

00:44:59.194 --> 00:45:02.340
I would say that when you around a boss or

00:45:02.340 --> 00:45:06.650
colleague, you're fast and
got lots of energy and excitement.

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:09.870
It rubs off on you and when you're
around people who are down, it rubs off.

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:12.890
So energy is the ability
to work could not sleep.

00:45:12.890 --> 00:45:17.470
I mean, the ability to have a lot
of intensity to what you do

00:45:17.470 --> 00:45:18.670
brings I think something.

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:23.950
And the fifth thing that I would talk
about is the ability, in the book

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:30.920
I talk about it, in the Power book I talk
about is the ability to tolerate conflict.

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:31.910
But it's more than that.

00:45:31.910 --> 00:45:33.290
It's the ability to not be right.

00:45:33.290 --> 00:45:37.400
So I have a friend who I talk about
a lot in the power book whose

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:41.970
just been named by TIME magazine as 100
most influential people in the world.

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:46.595
Woman who is changing medicine and
treats breast cancer very successfully.

00:45:46.595 --> 00:45:50.260
And I was in a meeting with her once,
and she's tough.

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:54.340
She's a little nicer now than she used
to be, but tough and not very patient.

00:45:54.340 --> 00:46:00.250
And somebody said you know, Laura,
I don't like your personality.

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:02.810
And you know she said when 45,000 women

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:06.190
a year are not dying of breast cancer
we can talk about my personality.

00:46:06.190 --> 00:46:10.539
And until then shut I'm going to
choose some excellent him up.

00:46:10.539 --> 00:46:17.100
[LAUGH] The point being, if you're trying
to achieve important changes and do

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:22.360
things that nobody else has done before,
you will almost invariably upset somebody.

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:28.430
Because the status quo remains the status
quo because people don't have interest.

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:31.460
And it remained in the status quo, and
so you're going to have to change it,

00:46:31.460 --> 00:46:33.910
people are going to get upset,
you can't let them worry you.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:40.280
&gt;&gt; Thank you, so
one other question is around leaders who

00:46:40.280 --> 00:46:43.950
are focused on their personal success
versus their organizational success,

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:46.150
it's perhaps tied into
the incentive question.

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:49.800
How can organizations,
perhaps channel that, personal

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:55.470
success drive of leaders to their benefit.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:59.710
&gt;&gt; Well, the topic of incentive
alignment would take us a whole other

00:46:59.710 --> 00:47:00.790
hour to talk about.

00:47:00.790 --> 00:47:04.960
But let me suggest that when
you see individuals acting in

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:09.350
their own selfish self interest and
against the interests of the organization.

00:47:09.350 --> 00:47:12.450
Besides blaming the individual and hoping
somehow, you're going to get rid of that

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:15.250
person and bring in somebody else
who's going to be different.

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:17.720
You really need to understand that,
that this is a system's problem.

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:20.710
That the organization is producing that
behavior, you want the behavior to change,

00:47:20.710 --> 00:47:23.350
you need to change the environment
in which that person is working.

00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:25.900
You need to change the cultural messages.

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:27.740
You probably need to
change the incentives.

00:47:27.740 --> 00:47:29.140
You probably need to change.

00:47:29.140 --> 00:47:30.930
What's cueing that individual.

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:34.390
Maybe you need to change the person
that the individual is reporting to.

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:37.380
Because oftentimes we say this
person is acting like blah,

00:47:37.380 --> 00:47:39.680
how is that individual's boss acting?

00:47:39.680 --> 00:47:40.620
Often the same way.

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:44.310
We're modeling the behavior, so
we can't just say good or bad person.

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:45.940
We need to do something
about that individual.

00:47:45.940 --> 00:47:47.960
We need to look at the forces,

00:47:47.960 --> 00:47:51.230
the messages that are getting sent
that are reinforcing that behavior.

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:56.090
&gt;&gt; So perhaps in closing,
one last question.

00:47:56.090 --> 00:47:59.090
This whole hour,
we've been talking about leaders.

00:47:59.090 --> 00:48:02.960
Is there any advice that
you'd give to followers?

00:48:02.960 --> 00:48:03.915
&gt;&gt; Become leaders.

00:48:03.915 --> 00:48:06.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean that we live in a hierarchical

00:48:06.290 --> 00:48:11.590
world, the evidence is clear in a book
written by the British epidemiologist

00:48:11.590 --> 00:48:16.120
Michael Marmot,
health follows the state is gradient.

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:18.500
The more education, the more wealth,
the higher position,

00:48:18.500 --> 00:48:21.780
the longer your likely to live.

00:48:21.780 --> 00:48:25.710
So yes, I think people in a in a
hierarchical world in which there's income

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:28.020
inequality, there's health inequality,

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:32.010
there's access to everything
that's reasonably unequal.

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:35.490
You need to decide where you
want to be in that distribution,

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:37.544
but if you want to be at the top
then you should go for it.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:41.310
Leaders by the way are not born,
they are made.

00:48:41.310 --> 00:48:43.330
Everything that we have
talked about today and

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:47.280
a bunch of stuff we haven't talked about
today are skills that can be learned.

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:53.000
And I think Alison, one of the reasons why
I'm so thrilled by Alison and her career.

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:59.270
And where she's gone and
how successful she's been is, she provides

00:48:59.270 --> 00:49:04.640
I think a case illustration of the point
that if Alison was painfully shy.

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:11.310
The word painfully shy was probably
written, the phrase, about her.

00:49:11.310 --> 00:49:15.160
If she can go from there that two
being able to give talks in front of

00:49:15.160 --> 00:49:16.770
hundreds of people and.

00:49:16.770 --> 00:49:19.470
You know, the senior corporate executives.

00:49:19.470 --> 00:49:23.200
We haul out the big
deal provost search and

00:49:23.200 --> 00:49:27.760
get offered university presidencies and
other deanships all the time.

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:29.840
This is something, this is personal,

00:49:29.840 --> 00:49:34.720
this is a journey,
a personal transformation and growth.

00:49:34.720 --> 00:49:37.920
But it is a journey and everybody's
journey obviously is going to be distinct

00:49:37.920 --> 00:49:41.290
But everyone can make their own journey
to become what they want to become.

00:49:41.290 --> 00:49:44.340
I want to thank Jeffery Pfeffer again for

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:50.300
this really insightful presentation and
I wish all of you a very good day.

00:49:50.300 --> 00:49:50.870
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:49:50.870 --> 00:49:52.009
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

